239476	239648	239472	239472	he	391	“ That letter , as I said , ought to suffice to prevent false impressions concerning you . But I am bound to tell you , Miss Tulliver , that not only the experience of my whole life , but my observation within the last three days , makes me fear that there is hardly any evidence which will save you from the painful effect of false imputations . The persons who are the most incapable of a conscientious struggle such as yours are precisely those who will be likely to shrink from you , because they will not believe in your struggle . I fear your life here will be attended not only with much pain , but with many obstructions . For this reason — and for this only — I ask you to consider whether it will not perhaps be better for you to take a situation at a distance , according to your former intention . I will exert myself at once to obtain one for you . ”
